WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.720
How could Jane Foster choose a life of superheroics
over her lifelong passion?

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.210
Was this magical weapon always available to
those who don't prove themselves worthy?

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.700
And what makes Thor a hero instead of just
a god?

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.690
These are the confusing moments in "Thor:
Love and Thunder" explained.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.960
Major spoilers ahead.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.091
Gorr and his daughter are the first people
seen in "Thor: Love and Thunder," as they

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:29.290
walk through a barren desert, dying of starvation
and thirst.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:34.989
All of this sets up the story of how Gorr
loses his faith and becomes the God Butcher.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.780
However, because Gorr and his daughter appear
to be human, viewers may be confused as to

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.070
whether the scene takes place on Earth.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.820
While the movie doesn't provide an explanation,
the comics offer some clues.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:53.079
In the comics, Gorr is more obviously alien
in his design, and his loss of faith explicitly

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:55.460
happens on an alien planet.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.830
With that, we can safely assume that movie
Gorr is also an alien.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.920
When the prologue takes place is more difficult
to answer.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.220
"Love and Thunder" leaves this to viewers'
imaginations.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:11.390
However in the comics, Gorr lost his faith
thousands of years ago, and even meets Thor

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.490
for the first time in the Middle Ages.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:18.610
While Thor doesn't encounter Gorr until the
present day in the movie, based on the comics,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.310
it's fair to believe that Gorr has been on
his god-killing quest for centuries, if not

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.200
millennia.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.680
After Hela shattered Thor's hammer Mjolnir
into pieces at the beginning of "Ragnarok,"

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.020
Thor needed a new weapon to take on Thanos
in "Infinity War."

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.230
So with the help of some of the Guardians
of the Galaxy, he traveled to Nidavellir to

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.360
ask Eitri the Dwarf to forge Stormbreaker
for him.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.290
Thor and his allies then heroically flew in
mid-battle to join the fight against Thanos

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.670
on Earth, and Thor demonstrated the awesome
power of his shiny new weapon.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.220
Stormbreaker remains Thor's weapon of choice
in his latest adventure, but when Jane shows

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.860
up wielding Mjolnir, Stormbreaker exhibits
all the signs of being jealous of Thor's close

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.170
relationship with his ex-weapon.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:09.390
While it's a hilarious take on a love triangle,
some viewers may find themselves wondering

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.489
why Stormbreaker seems sentient while Mjolnir
never did.

00:02:13.489 --> 00:02:18.439
The answer has been supplied by "Thor: Love
and Thunder" director Taika Waititi, who pointed

00:02:18.439 --> 00:02:22.150
out that the ax's handle was made out of Groot's
arm.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:26.609
Groot is currently a teenager, making his
former arm a teenager too.

00:02:26.609 --> 00:02:30.430
Because of its connection to Groot, not only
does Stormbreaker have a certain level of

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.430
consciousness, like many teenagers, it's also
fairly temperamental.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.730
Jane and Thor fell in love during 2011's "Thor,"
but after breaking the Bifrost, they didn't

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.639
see each other again until two years later
in "Thor: The Dark World."

00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:50.430
After that, Jane and Thor embarked on a romance,
but as Korg explains in "Love and Thunder,"

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.840
soon fear and their commitment to their jobs
led to their break-up.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.510
"Sorry to hear that Jane dumped you."

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.420
"She didn't dump me, I dumped her.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.719
It was a mutual dumping."

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:05.480
While it's not entirely clear when this happened,
we know Jane and Thor were no longer together

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:10.019
by "Ragnarok," and their reunion offers an
opportunity for further clarity.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:14.519
When Thor and Jane meet again, Jane casually
asks how long they've been apart.

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:17.129
"What's it been, three, four years?"

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.920
"Eight years, seven months and six days, give
or take."

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.559
Some fans may be confused about why the discrepancy
between their recollections of the timing

00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:28.909
of their break-up is so different, but there
are a couple of potential explanations.

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:33.400
On the one hand, it points to the fact that
Thor has been literally counting the days

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.699
since they parted, while Jane has had other
things on her mind.

00:03:36.699 --> 00:03:41.199
That would indicate Thor's been holding a
much bigger and brighter torch for Jane than

00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:42.510
she has for him.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:47.010
However, Jane's belief that it's been somewhere
between three and four years might suggest

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.620
she may have been snapped away by Thanos — meaning
that in her mind, their break-up is far more

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.900
recent than it is for Thor because, for five
of the eight-plus years since, she technically

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:57.939
didn't exist.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:02.799
Is it a subtle nod to Thanos' actions or are
we reading too much into things?

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:04.069
You decide.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:09.249
"Thor: Love and Thunder" introduces several
new locations, but perhaps the most impressive

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:14.109
is Omnipotence City, an embassy for gods of
all kinds from across the universe.

00:04:14.109 --> 00:04:18.669
Yet, while all gods are welcome there in the
comics, that isn't the case in the film.

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:22.289
Thor indicates that their attendance at the
meeting of gods where they plan to ask for

00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:26.190
Zeus' help to take on Gorr is by invitation
only.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.510
In fact, they're so concerned about getting
kicked out before they can make their request,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.630
that they don the clothes of a set of gods
that were invited as a disguise.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:39.819
Still, it's hard not to wonder why Thor hasn't
been invited to Omnipotence City before.

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:44.720
After all, he's a god from a pretty powerful
family that's made waves across the Nine Realms.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.639
Moreover, in the comics, Thor once went to
Omnipotence City to seek information about

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:52.410
Gorr's whereabouts from its vast library.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:57.520
Yet, while his destination in the movie is
the same, Thor's not exactly welcome in the

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.590
MCU's version of Omnipotence City, In fact,
while Thor may seem like a big deal in other

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:08.810
contexts, Zeus indicates he doesn't think
too highly of the space Viking, noting that

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.710
once Odin died, he was under the impression
his whole pantheon was gone.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:17.500
In the comics, the denizens of Olympus and
Asgard are usually friends and allies.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.210
Of course, there are always exceptions, but
it seems that there's not a whole lot of goodwill

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.060
between the two pantheons in the MCU.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.590
Perhaps that's why Thor has never landed an
Omnipotence City invite before.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.659
When we first met Thor more than a decade
ago, he reveled in war and sought out fights

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:36.270
even when there was no need.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:41.189
"Through your arrogance and stupidity, you
have opened this peaceful realms and innocent

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:45.759
lives to the horror and desolation of war."

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:50.550
But after Odin stripped him of Mjolnir and
banished him to Earth, Thor learned his lesson:

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.380
His ability to help people was his true superpower.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:58.330
It was this realization that led him to join
the Avengers and become a defender of Earth

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.330
and its people.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.069
Yet, the other gods we meet in "Love and Thunder"
don't seem to share Thor's concern for the

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:05.069
greater good.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:10.030
In "Love and Thunder," the gods come across
as vain and complacent and they believe mortal

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:12.749
beings' only purpose is to worship them.

00:06:12.749 --> 00:06:14.250
This is a bit confusing.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.510
After all, Thor's nothing like these other
gods.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:20.599
Part of the reason for this may be simply
that Thor's Viking background means he was

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:25.889
an especially talented fighter and it gave
him a skill that he could use to benefit others,

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:28.669
a skill that many other gods may not share.

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:33.469
That said, gods like Zeus used to be powerful
forces on Earth but aren't anymore.

00:06:33.469 --> 00:06:38.240
It's possible that just as their worshippers
have rejected them, they've turned against

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.770
their former worshippers.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:44.350
However, it's more likely that Thor is so
different from the other Gods directly because

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:46.509
of his humbling experiences on Earth.

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.949
Once he realized that human beings could be
worth protecting, he became a true hero, instead

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:52.080
of just a god.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.379
It doesn't seem as if the other gods in Omnipotence
City have had the same humbling moments.

00:06:58.379 --> 00:07:03.860
When Thor gets the attention of Zeus in Omnipotence
City and asks him to raise an army of gods

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:09.070
to help him take on Gorr, Zeus is unwilling
to help, arguing that the gods are safe where

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.070
they are.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.659
The only measure he's willing to take to protect
the gathered deities from Gorr is to force

00:07:14.659 --> 00:07:19.729
Thor and his allies to stay there so Gorr
can't learn the location of Omnipotence City

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:20.729
from them.

00:07:20.729 --> 00:07:24.939
Yet, some fans may scratch their heads at
this, not because Zeus' reason for holding

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:29.969
Thor is flawed but because if he wants to
keep Thor in Omnipotence City, he should want

00:07:29.969 --> 00:07:33.330
to do the same with every other god in existence.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:38.210
After all, if Thor knows where Omnipotence
City is, it stands to reason that all the

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:39.830
other gods do too.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.279
So even if Zeus doesn't want to take on Gorr,
if he wants to ensure none of the gods Gorr

00:07:44.279 --> 00:07:49.879
goes after reveals the location of Omnipotence
City, it seems he should send someone to gather

00:07:49.879 --> 00:07:52.330
up any gods who aren't already there.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:57.610
That way the gods will all be safe in Omnipotence
City and Gorr will never learn their location.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:02.670
It's a win-win, and since Thor is the only
god who seems to want to confront Gorr, Zeus

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:07.569
probably wouldn't get much pushback to keeping
all the gods in the City.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:11.779
When Zeus tries to have his soldiers take
Thor and his friends into custody so they

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:15.749
can't leave Omnipotence City, it leads to
a brutal brawl.

00:08:15.749 --> 00:08:21.689
Yet, despite all the gods surrounding them,
the only one that helps the soldiers is Zeus.

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:27.029
Zeus wreaks plenty of havoc with his weapon,
Thunderbolt, including almost obliterating

00:08:27.029 --> 00:08:28.029
Korg.

00:08:28.029 --> 00:08:31.880
When he attempts to take Thor out with it,
though, Thor catches it and throws it back

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:37.370
at Zeus, seemingly landing a killing blow
to the shock of the other gods watching.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:41.899
Despite appearing to meet his demise, however,
it's revealed in a mid-credits scene that

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:45.240
although Zeus was wounded, he's far from deceased.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.930
This could lead some to wonder if Zeus can
truly die at all.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:53.810
Zeus is still alive in the comics, so there's
no definitive evidence one way or another.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:58.949
Plus, we know that, regardless of how poetic
it might have been, Zeus' own weapon can't

00:08:58.949 --> 00:09:02.490
kill him, which likely means no other divine
weapon can either.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:07.589
However, even though Zeus is unwilling to
seek out Gorr for the good of gods everywhere,

00:09:07.589 --> 00:09:11.620
his attempt to prevent Thor from going after
him so Gorr can't discover the location of

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.300
Omnipotence City indicates he's scared of
Gorr's weapon, the Necrosword.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:22.199
So while it may be almost impossible for Zeus
to die, it seems there's at least one weapon

00:09:22.199 --> 00:09:24.870
in the universe that can kill him.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:29.870
After Gorr takes up the Necrosword it grants
him all kinds of powers that ironically mimic

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.380
those of a deity.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.810
Gorr is immortal as long as the sword exists,
has enhanced strength that allows him to overcome

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.149
gods, and is able to create shadow monsters
that do his bidding.

00:09:40.149 --> 00:09:44.930
Yet, it's only when Thor, Mighty Thor, and
Valkyrie reach the Shadow realm where Gorr

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:51.070
resides that we see he possesses one more
unexpected skill: the ability to read minds.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:55.899
When the trio initially arrives, the God Butcher
is nowhere to be found, and when he finally

00:09:55.899 --> 00:10:00.510
appears he's like something out of a horror
movie as he immobilizes them and then taunts

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:04.000
them with their darkest memories, impulses,
and feelings.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.360
While his ability to use each individuals'
own thoughts against them makes Gorr even

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:13.250
scarier than he already was, it's hard not
to wonder where this particular power comes

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:14.250
from.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:19.130
In the comics, the Necrosword granted Gorr
plenty of abilities, but telepathy wasn't

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:20.150
one of them.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:24.860
Yet, earlier in "Love and Thunder" the Necrosword
appears to have the power to not only call

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:30.230
out to Gorr but to know that he would be a
worthy successor to the individual who previously

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:31.230
wielded it.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.630
This indicates that, at least in the MCU,
the Necrosword grants its user the ability

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.830
to see into others' minds.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:42.400
It may not be the most useful skill when your
goal is to slay gods, but it's sure freaky

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.130
when Gorr uses it to torture Thor and his
allies in the movie.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:48.139
"Where were you?"

00:10:48.139 --> 00:10:50.160
"Where were you?"

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.950
Outside of a brief appearance in "Endgame,"
Jane hasn't been seen in the MCU in almost

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:59.640
a decade, and when we catch up with her in
"Love and Thunder," she isn't in a good place.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.190
Jane has advanced-stage cancer, and even though
she's getting treated for it, it isn't getting

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.190
better.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:09.570
Nonetheless, Jane continues to work, even
sleeping in her lab so she can get more done.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:14.060
While the movie suggests that part of what
Jane is doing centers on finding a cure for

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:18.050
her cancer, it's reasonable to assume she's
still pursuing the astrophysics work that

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:21.510
was established as her passion early in the
"Thor" franchise.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:26.829
Yet, after she travels to Asgard, reconnects
with Mjolnir, and becomes the Mighty Thor,

00:11:26.829 --> 00:11:31.820
Jane completely abandons her life's work to
travel across the cosmos and fight Gorr.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.509
While the movie doesn't put too fine a point
on it, some viewers may feel this seems out

00:11:35.509 --> 00:11:41.020
of character for Jane, whose brilliant mind
balanced Thor's spectacular brawn in the first

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.610
two "Thor" films.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:46.709
Ultimately, the reason behind her changed
focus is supplied by Jane herself.

00:11:46.709 --> 00:11:52.100
The cancer is making Jane feel weak and incapable,
but picking up Mjolnir and becoming the Mighty

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.550
Thor makes her feel strong and powerful.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.110
To Jane, it's like she's been granted another
life.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:02.009
So if her choices are only to continue to
pursue her physics work until she's too weak

00:12:02.009 --> 00:12:07.160
to do so anymore or to periodically feel strong
as the Mighty Thor, it's understandable why

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.370
she'd choose the second option.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.170
"Let's bring the rainbow."

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:16.769
Throughout its time in the MCU, Mjolnir has
never demonstrated the same level of sentience

00:12:16.769 --> 00:12:19.350
that Stormbreaker does in "Love and Thunder."

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.529
Nonetheless, it's clear Mjolnir knows how
to listen.

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:26.829
Not only does it refuse to let the God of
Thunder reclaim it on Odin's orders in the

00:12:26.829 --> 00:12:31.709
first "Thor" film, but in his fourth solo
adventure when Thor tells Mjolnir to always

00:12:31.709 --> 00:12:35.800
protect Jane while the pair are a couple,
the hammer clearly takes the instructions

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:36.800
to heart.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.269
So when Jane's cancer doesn't appear to be
improving, Mjolnir calls to her from New Asgard,

00:12:42.269 --> 00:12:46.920
and once she arrives, it puts its shattered
remains back together and grants her the power

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:47.920
of Thor.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.600
Yet, it soon becomes clear that wielding Mjolnir
is actually accelerating Jane's cancer.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:59.400
As a doctor on New Asgard tells Thor, Jane
is rapidly declining, and it seems her taking

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.330
up the mantle of the Mighty Thor is the reason.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:04.769
But this is a confusing revelation.

00:13:04.769 --> 00:13:09.110
After all, if Mjolnir's goal is to protect
Jane, how is it possible that it's actually

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.420
making things worse for her?

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.510
Unfortunately, Mjolnir's options for helping
Jane are pretty much limited to granting her

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.310
superhero powers.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:22.160
In the comics, when Jane uses Mjolnir to become
the Mighty Thor, it removes all toxins from

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:27.190
her body including the chemotherapy and other
drugs treating her cancer, while leaving cancer

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:29.329
behind because it's a part of her.

00:13:29.329 --> 00:13:34.480
So while it's doing the best it can to keep
Jane safe, Mjolnir doesn't realize its form

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.899
of protection won't actually do much to assist
a terminally ill human.

00:13:38.899 --> 00:13:42.971
It's a reasonable oversight when you're used
to being wielded by space Vikings, but it

00:13:42.971 --> 00:13:46.070
hastens Jane's sad end.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:50.089
Ever since Thor was introduced in the MCU,
a major part of his story has centered on

00:13:50.089 --> 00:13:53.120
whether he was worthy of wielding Mjolnir.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.529
As a result, it's understandable that when
he was forced to switch weapons, many fans

00:13:57.529 --> 00:14:03.449
believed that same worthiness clause applied
to his newly forged ax Stormbreaker.

00:14:03.449 --> 00:14:08.220
After all, Stormbreaker certainly seems to
be as powerful as Mjolnir in many ways.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:13.180
So these same fans may be very confused when
the seemingly unworthy Gorr is able to take

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:17.350
Stormbreaker from Thor and carry it off with
him in "Love and Thunder."

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:22.740
But it won't be the first time fans have wondered
if characters have to be worthy to lift Stormbreaker.

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:27.779
The question was raised after Groot lifted
the ax to offer his arm as a handle in "Infinity

00:14:27.779 --> 00:14:30.960
War," with that film's directors Joe and Anthony
Russo clarifying

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.160
"Mjolnir requires worthiness, not Stormbreaker."

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:39.509
In fact, even Mjolnir didn't require worthiness
until Odin whispered the words that gave it

00:14:39.509 --> 00:14:40.639
this property.

00:14:40.639 --> 00:14:44.329
Considering that, it makes sense that pretty
much anyone can lift Stormbreaker if they

00:14:44.329 --> 00:14:45.920
want to.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:50.860
The Necrosword has granted Gorr an unnaturally
long life, but that longevity has come with

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:56.210
a trade-off: If the Necrosword is ever destroyed,
Gorr will die quickly afterward.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:15:01.110
So when Thor returns to the Shadow realm to
rescue Asgard's children and once again attempt

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:05.230
to defeat Gorr, his main goal is to shatter
the Necrosword.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:10.300
Thor manages to get this done with an assist
from the kidnapped Asgardian kids who he temporarily

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:15.759
shares his powers with, and Jane, who he'd
begged to stay on New Asgard, as another fight

00:15:15.759 --> 00:15:18.389
as Mighty Thor is likely to kill her.

00:15:18.389 --> 00:15:23.300
Despite the demise of the Necrosword, Gorr
still opens the doors to Eternity before he

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:25.730
dies, which was his goal all along.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:30.130
In "Love and Thunder," Eternity is a being
at the center of the universe who will grant

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:33.089
the individual who finds it a single wish.

00:15:33.089 --> 00:15:37.139
And even though he's dying, Gorr still has
enough life in him to see his plan through

00:15:37.139 --> 00:15:38.410
to the end.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:44.600
Thor and Jane follow him into Eternity, where
Jane collapses, and Thor admits Gorr has won.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.040
Yet, it's not clear why it has to be that
way.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:53.000
After all, Gorr no longer has the power of
the Necrosword, so it seems Thor could easily

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.550
kill him before he made his wish, saving himself
in the process.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:16:01.850
Plus, with Gorr out of the way, presumably,
Gorr's wish would pass to Thor, and he could

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:07.740
wish Jane's cancer away, saving her and enabling
them to pursue their rekindled romance.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:12.899
Instead, the pair convinces Gorr to wish for
his daughter to live instead of all gods to

00:16:12.899 --> 00:16:17.140
die, which ensures Thor's survival, but leaves
Jane to perish.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:22.240
Why would Thor choose to spare the life of
his enemy instead of killing him in cold blood?

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.529
Well, as he says in Thor: Ragnarok,

00:16:24.529 --> 00:16:27.110
"Because that's what heroes do."

